1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,737
$10,000 of credit card debt.

2
00:00:03,157 --> 00:00:07,438
Let's run three real ways to pay it off, and see exactly what each one costs.

3
00:00:07,858 --> 00:00:12,650
At 24%, that's about $200 of interest in the first month alone.

4
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:14,955
Same debt. Three plans.

5
00:00:15,725 --> 00:00:17,722
Plan A. Three hundred dollars a month.

6
00:00:18,142 --> 00:00:23,132
That's 56 months. Almost five years. And $6,644 in interest.

7
00:00:23,552 --> 00:00:27,253
That's two-thirds of the original debt, paid again, just in interest.

8
00:00:28,023 --> 00:00:31,420
Plan B. Find two hundred more, and pay five hundred a month.

9
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:34,995
26 months, and $2,899 in interest.

10
00:00:35,415 --> 00:00:40,028
$200 more a month saves about $3,700, and two and a half years.

11
00:00:40,798 --> 00:00:45,046
Plan C: the same $500 a month, plus the money that shows up anyway.

12
00:00:45,466 --> 00:00:46,834
Here's what Plan C adds.

13
00:00:46,994 --> 00:00:49,274
A $2,000 tax refund in March.

14
00:00:49,434 --> 00:00:51,866
$1,000 from the extra paycheck in July.

15
00:00:52,026 --> 00:00:53,811
And another $1,000 from December's.

16
00:00:54,231 --> 00:00:55,506
Now, side by side.

17
00:00:55,926 --> 00:00:57,656
Plan B. Twenty-six months.

18
00:00:58,076 --> 00:01:01,656
Plan C: 16 months, and $1,543 in interest.

19
00:01:02,076 --> 00:01:05,448
Same debt. 40 months and $5,100 apart.

20
00:01:05,868 --> 00:01:10,277
So decide now where your windfalls go, before they show up and quietly get spent.

21
00:01:11,047 --> 00:01:17,651
Your tax refund will vary, so use your own numbers. The Paycheck Planner in the description gives you your debt-free date.

22
00:01:18,071 --> 00:01:23,186
Subscribe. Next time: why the minimum payment keeps you paying for almost seventeen years.
